<h1>PROJECT PROPOSAL</h1>
<h2>GadaSpace</h2>
<p><strong>A Multi-Detector Radiation Analysis, Data Fusion, Shielding Trade-Off Optimization, and Prediction System for Space Missions</strong></p>
<p>
<strong>Project Category:</strong> Space Technology / Software Innovation / Scientific Research<br>
<strong>Project Type:</strong> Software-Based Research and Development Prototype<br>
<strong>Prepared for:</strong> 2026 NASA International Space Apps Challenge<br>
<strong>Project Name:</strong> GadaSpace<br>
<strong>Team Name:</strong> Horizon-X<br>
<strong>Proposed Platform:</strong> Web-Based Application (FastAPI + Interactive Mission Control Dashboard)<br>
<strong>Date:</strong> November 2026
</p>
<hr>

<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>Space radiation is one of the important challenges facing astronauts and spacecraft during space missions. Different forms of radiation, including Galactic Cosmic Rays (GCRs), Solar Energetic Particles (SEPs), and radiation trapped in planetary magnetic fields, can expose spacecraft systems and astronauts to potentially harmful radiation.</p>
<p>Current radiation monitoring approaches use different types of detectors to measure radiation properties such as absorbed dose, particle energy, particle flux, and linear energy transfer (LET). However, individual detectors have limitations. Some measure energy deposition without identifying the original particle, while others may have limited sensitivity to certain radiation types or energy ranges.</p>
<p>GadaSpace is a proposed software platform developed by Team Horizon-X, designed to bring together data from different radiation detectors and scientific models to provide a more comprehensive analysis of radiation conditions inside a spacecraft.</p>
<p>The system will process available radiation datasets, validate and combine compatible measurements, incorporate particle transport simulation results, and present the findings through interactive visualizations and analytical dashboards.</p>
<p>The main goal is to investigate whether combining multiple detector measurements with physics-based modelling can improve radiation dose estimation and help distinguish primary radiation from secondary radiation.</p>
<p>Furthermore, to address real-world aerospace engineering and crew-health constraints, GadaSpace expands traditional radiation monitoring into a complete, six-stage operational workflow:</p>
<div class="formula-box">Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor astronaut exposure</div>
<p>Within this workflow, the platform evaluates pre-flight orbital trajectories through Earth's Van Allen radiation belts, compares planetary surface radiation environments on the Moon versus Mars, models the <strong>Secondary Radiation Paradox</strong> (spallation neutrons and Bremsstrahlung X-rays generated inside thick metal shields), and optimizes a novel <strong>3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z Shielding Architecture</strong> (combining an ultra-thin High-Z stripper, a Low-Z Polyethylene/Water moderator, and an inner secondary-radiation scavenger composed of <strong>Gadolinium-157 Doped Ti3C2Tx MXene Nanosheets</strong> and a <strong>Chernobyl-Derived Radiotrophic Fungal Melanin Hydrogel</strong>). Crucially, rather than optimizing for radiation protection in isolation, the system optimizes the fundamental four-way spacecraft engineering trade-off:</p>
<div class="formula-box">Protection &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Mass &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Power &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Crew safety</div>
<p>Finally, the system translates physical detector measurements into rigorous biological risk estimates—calculating physical Absorbed Dose (<strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gray) and applying Linear Energy Transfer (LET) quality weighting <strong>Q(LET)</strong> to estimate equivalent/effective dose in Sieverts (Sv) alongside cellular DNA damage indicators.</p>
<p>The project will begin as a research and educational prototype using available scientific datasets and simulated data. Its results will be evaluated against reference data to understand its accuracy, limitations, and potential value for future space radiation monitoring systems.</p>

<h2>2. Background and Introduction</h2>
<p>Space is not an environment free from radiation. Spacecraft and astronauts may encounter radiation originating from the Sun, distant cosmic sources, and planetary radiation environments.</p>
<p>The three major categories of space radiation include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Galactic Cosmic Rays (GCRs):</strong> High-energy particles originating outside the solar system (consisting of ~85% protons, ~14% alpha particles, and ~1% High-Z and High-Energy [HZE] heavy ions such as relativistic Iron-56, which possess extreme Linear Energy Transfer and biological destructiveness).</li>
  <li><strong>Solar Energetic Particles (SEPs):</strong> Energetic particles associated with solar activity, including solar eruptions, X-class solar flares, and fast Coronal Mass Ejections (CMEs), which produce acute, high-flux proton storms.</li>
  <li><strong>Trapped Radiation:</strong> Energetic particles held within planetary magnetic fields, such as Earth's Van Allen radiation belts—specifically the <strong>Inner Proton Belt</strong> (1,000–6,000 km altitude, E &gt; 100 MeV) and the <strong>Outer Relativistic Electron Belt</strong> (13,000–25,000 km altitude, E = 1–10 MeV)—which spacecraft must traverse during Trans-Lunar Injection (TLI) and orbital ascent.</li>
</ul>
<p>Beyond Earth's protective dipolar magnetosphere (~30,000–60,000 nT), planetary magnetic and atmospheric differences dramatically alter the surface radiation environment:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The Planetary Magnetic Deficit &amp; The Moon vs. Mars Surface Paradox:</strong> Neither the Moon nor Mars possesses a global intrinsic dipolar magnetic field. However, empirical measurements from NASA's LRO CRaTER and Curiosity MSL RAD reveal a counterintuitive reality: the unshielded lunar surface (~380–420 mSv/yr) is roughly 35% to 56% more radioactive than the Martian surface (~240–300 mSv/yr). Because the Moon is an airless vacuum (0 g/cm2 atmosphere, ~0 nT global field), primary GCRs strike the silicate regolith unimpeded and splash upward as dangerous <strong>albedo neutron backscatter</strong> (~18% additional dose). Mars, by contrast, provides natural overhead shielding via its CO2 atmosphere (~20 g/cm2 average column density, rising to ~34.5 g/cm2 inside deep basins like Hellas Planitia) and localized crustal remanent magnetic anomalies (up to ~1,500 nT in regions such as Terra Sirenum) that screen low-energy solar protons.</li>
</ul>
<p>When these particles interact with spacecraft structures and shielding materials, they can produce secondary particles. As a result, the radiation environment inside a spacecraft may differ significantly from the environment outside it.</p>
<p>In aerospace physics, this phenomenon is known as the <strong>Secondary Radiation Paradox</strong>: when relativistic heavy ions (such as 56Fe) or energetic electrons strike thick high-atomic-number (High-Z) metals such as Lead (Z = 82) or Iron (Z = 26), they shatter atomic nuclei via <strong>nuclear spallation</strong> (releasing shower bursts of uncharged fast secondary neutrons) and decelerate rapidly to emit <strong>Bremsstrahlung (braking radiation) X-rays</strong>. Consequently, an improperly designed heavy metal shield can actually increase the biological radiation dose inside the spacecraft cabin while imposing an unacceptable launch mass penalty.</p>
<p>Radiation detectors provide important measurements, but no single detector necessarily captures every property needed to fully characterize the radiation environment.</p>
<p>This creates an opportunity to develop a system that combines multiple sources of information rather than relying on one measurement alone.</p>
<p>GadaSpace aims to explore this opportunity through data integration, scientific modelling, multi-objective shielding optimization, and interactive analysis.</p>

<h2>3. Problem Statement</h2>
<p>Radiation monitoring in space involves multiple detector technologies, each with different measurement capabilities, limitations, and response characteristics.</p>
<p>For example, a detector may measure absorbed energy without reliably identifying whether the detected radiation came directly from space or was produced through interactions with spacecraft materials.</p>
<p>Other detectors may provide particle-related information but have limitations in energy coverage, sensitivity, or spatial resolution.</p>
<p>These differences create several challenges:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Fragmented measurements:</strong> Radiation information may be distributed across different instruments and datasets.</li>
  <li><strong>Limited particle identification:</strong> Some measurements cannot independently determine the origin or type of the detected radiation.</li>
  <li><strong>Primary and secondary radiation (The Secondary Radiation Paradox):</strong> Interactions between incoming particles and spacecraft materials can create secondary radiation (spallation fast neutrons and Bremsstrahlung X-rays) that complicates analysis and can make naive high-Z metal shields counterproductive.</li>
  <li><strong>Different measurement formats:</strong> Detector datasets may use different units, formats, timestamps, and calibration methods.</li>
  <li><strong>Limited spatial understanding:</strong> Measurements from individual detector locations may not describe the entire spacecraft environment, orbital transit path, or planetary surface site.</li>
  <li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> Detector limitations, missing measurements, and modelling assumptions can affect the reliability of radiation estimates.</li>
  <li><strong>Pre-flight Van Allen Belt trajectory exposure:</strong> Mission planners need quantitative tools to evaluate how orbital inclination and Trans-Lunar Injection (TLI) transit duration through the Inner Proton and Outer Electron belts affect cumulative crew and avionics dose prior to leaving Earth orbit.</li>
  <li><strong>Planetary surface asymmetry (Moon vs. Mars):</strong> Mission architectures must account for the stark differences between the Moon's airless regolith neutron-albedo environment and Mars's CO2 atmospheric attenuation and localized crustal magnetic fields.</li>
  <li><strong>The Spacecraft Multi-Objective Engineering Constraint:</strong> A spacecraft cannot optimize only for radiation protection. Every kilogram of shielding mass reduces payload capacity and requires exponential rocket propellant, while active systems consume scarce electrical power. Engineering solutions must explicitly optimize:
    <div class="formula-box">Protection &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Mass &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Power &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Crew safety</div>
    For example, if Shield A reduces radiation by 40% but adds 500 kg, while Shield B reduces radiation by 32% but adds only 100 kg, Shield B may actually be the superior spacecraft engineering solution.</li>
  <li><strong>Rigorous Estimation of Astronaut Exposure and Biological Risk:</strong> Rather than vaguely claiming to "measure how much radiation affected the astronaut," a scientifically defensible system must <em>estimate the astronaut's radiation exposure and associated biological risk</em> by computing physical Absorbed Dose (<strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gy) and integrating Linear Energy Transfer (LET) radiation quality factors <strong>Q(LET)</strong> to derive Equivalent/Effective Dose in Sieverts (Sv).</li>
</ol>
<p>The central problem addressed by this project is:</p>
<p><strong>How can measurements from multiple radiation detectors be combined with particle transport modelling, multi-objective shielding trade-off analysis, and LET-weighted bio-dosimetry to provide a more complete and understandable analysis of radiation exposure, protection, and astronaut biological risk inside a spacecraft and across planetary surfaces?</strong></p>

<h2>4. Proposed Solution</h2>
<p>Team Horizon-X proposes <strong>GadaSpace</strong>, a software-based radiation analysis, prediction, shielding optimization, and bio-dosimetry platform that combines available detector measurements with physics-based simulation results across a closed-loop operational pipeline:</p>
<div class="formula-box">Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor astronaut exposure</div>
<p>The system will have eight main capabilities:</p>

<h3>4.1 Multi-Detector Data Integration (Detect)</h3>
<p>The platform will accept radiation datasets from different detector technologies, including:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Silicon detectors (e.g., solid-state telescope channels on NASA LRO CRaTER, Curiosity MSL RAD, and Van Allen Probes MagEIS/REPT)</li>
  <li>Tissue-Equivalent Proportional Counters (TEPC) and Tissue-Equivalent Plastic (TEP) dosimeters</li>
  <li>Scintillation detectors (e.g., CsI/plastic scintillators for gamma and charged-particle spectroscopy)</li>
  <li>Neutron detectors (measuring both spacecraft secondary spallation neutrons and planetary regolith albedo neutrons)</li>
  <li>Passive dosimeters, where suitable data is available</li>
</ul>
<p>The platform will identify measurement types, units, timestamps, detector information, and available calibration metadata.</p>
<p>It will validate incoming datasets and identify missing or inconsistent information before analysis.</p>

<h3>4.2 Primary and Secondary Radiation Analysis (Characterize)</h3>
<p>The system will investigate the contribution of primary and secondary radiation.</p>
<p>Primary radiation refers to incoming energetic particles from the space environment (GCR protons, HZE heavy ions such as 56Fe, and SEP solar protons). Secondary radiation refers to particles produced when incoming radiation interacts with spacecraft materials or planetary regolith—specifically <strong>spallation fast neutrons</strong> and <strong>Bremsstrahlung X-rays</strong>.</p>
<p>GadaSpace will use detector information and particle transport modelling (calibrated against NASA OLTARIS / HZETRN-2020 nuclear fragmentation cross-sections) to estimate these contributions where the available data and model support such analysis.</p>
<p>The platform will clearly distinguish direct measurements from model-derived estimates.</p>

<h3>4.3 Radiation Data Fusion &amp; Orbital/Planetary Mapping (Map)</h3>
<p>Instead of analyzing each detector independently, the system will investigate how compatible measurements can be combined.</p>
<p>A data-fusion approach may use statistical techniques such as inverse-variance spectral weighting, Bayesian estimation, or Kalman filtering, depending on the data characteristics and assumptions.</p>
<p>In addition to cabin sensor fusion, the system maps radiation across:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pre-Flight Van Allen Belt Trajectories:</strong> Integrating proton and relativistic electron flux across L-shells (L = 1.1 to 6.5 RE) based on launch inclination and Trans-Lunar Injection (TLI) transit time.</li>
  <li><strong>Lunar vs. Martian Surface Environments:</strong> Mapping lunar surface exposure (~380–420 mSv/yr with regolith albedo neutron backscatter vs. sheltered lunar lava tubes) against Martian surface sites (~240–300 mSv/yr, incorporating CO2 atmospheric column density attenuation from Gale Crater to Hellas Planitia and localized crustal magnetic fields in Terra Sirenum).</li>
</ul>
<p>The purpose is to investigate whether combining different measurements can provide more informative estimates than relying on a single detector.</p>

<h3>4.4 Short-Term Space Weather &amp; Dose Prediction (Predict)</h3>
<p>Using solar wind precursors (proton flux channels &gt;10, &gt;50, &gt;100 MeV, X-ray flare flux, interplanetary magnetic field Bz, and CME transit speeds from NOAA GOES / NASA OMNIWeb), the system provides short-term (1 to 72-hour) forecasting of Solar Energetic Particle (SEP) storm escalation, enabling proactive EVA abort warnings and storm-shelter alerts before peak dose arrival.</p>

<h3>4.5 Multi-Objective Shielding Optimization &amp; Novel 3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Architecture (Protect)</h3>
<p>NASA's own radiation-protection approach emphasizes minimizing crew exposure within mission design constraints, rather than simply adding unlimited shielding. Therefore, GadaSpace explicitly optimizes:</p>
<div class="formula-box">Protection (%) &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Mass (kg) &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Power (kW) &nbsp;&nbsp;vs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Crew safety</div>
<p>To defeat the Secondary Radiation Paradox while minimizing launch mass and power draw, GadaSpace models and optimizes a novel <strong>3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z Shielding Architecture</strong> alongside standard aerospace materials:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Outer Layer — Ultra-Thin High-Z / Mid-Z HZE Stripper (&lt; 1.0 g/cm2 Thin Lead or Aerospace Aluminum):</strong> Strips orbital electrons and fragments incoming relativistic heavy ions (56Fe) into lower-Z nuclei while remaining thin enough to avoid triggering runaway Bremsstrahlung X-ray or spallation neutron cascades.</li>
  <li><strong>Middle Layer — Low-Z Hydrogenous Moderator (5.0–6.0 g/cm2 High-Density Polyethylene [HDPE, C2H4] + Water [H2O]):</strong> Packed with hydrogen atoms to maximize elastic proton-proton recoil, rapidly decelerating primary protons and moderating fast neutrons without producing secondary nuclear fragments.</li>
  <li><strong>Inner Layer — Novel Bio-Nanocomposite Secondary Radiation Scavenger (1.5–2.0 g/cm2):</strong> Absorbs residual thermalized neutrons and Bremsstrahlung X-rays using two frontier materials never previously combined in hackathon radiation tools:
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gadolinium-157 Doped Ti3C2Tx MXene Nanosheet (<code>Gd157_MXene_Nanosheet</code>):</strong> Exploits the <strong>255,000-barn thermal neutron capture cross-section</strong> of 157Gd—the highest of any stable isotope—intercalated into 2D titanium carbide MXene nanosheets to capture slowed neutrons and attenuate secondary gamma rays at a fraction of the mass of traditional shields.</li>
      <li><strong>Chernobyl-Derived Radiotrophic Fungal Melanin Biopolymer Hydrogel (<code>Melanin_Biopolymer_Hydrogel</code>):</strong> Inspired by radiotrophic fungi (<em>Cryptococcus neoformans</em> and <em>Cladosporium sphaerospermum</em>) tested aboard the ISS, this high-hydrogen melanin hydrogel absorbs low-energy Bremsstrahlung X-rays via conjugated aromatic pi-electron rings, scavenges ionizing free radicals, operates at <strong>0 W active electrical power</strong>, and offers self-healing, in-situ biomanufacturing potential.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ol>
<p>The platform quantitatively compares canonical spacecraft trade-offs—demonstrating, for instance, why <strong>Shield A</strong> (40% dose reduction at +500 kg mass penalty) is inferior to <strong>Shield B</strong> (32% dose reduction at +100 kg mass penalty) on a mass-constrained spacecraft, and how <strong>Shield C (3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z)</strong> achieves <strong>~61.5% net dose reduction at only +145 kg and ~0.05 kW power</strong>.</p>

<h3>4.6 Astronaut Exposure &amp; Biological Risk Estimation (Monitor)</h3>
<p>Rather than loosely claiming to measure how much radiation affected an astronaut, GadaSpace is rigorously designed to <strong>estimate the astronaut's radiation exposure and associated biological risk</strong>:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Absorbed Dose (D):</strong> Calculates physical energy deposited per unit mass:
    <div class="formula-box">D = E / m &nbsp;&nbsp;[in Gray, where 1 Gy = 1 J/kg]</div>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Radiation Quality Factor Q(LET):</strong> Accounts for radiation quality using Linear Energy Transfer (LET, in keV/um) per NASA and ICRP-60/103 standards—recognizing that high-LET heavy ions and secondary fragments inflict far greater biological damage per Gray than low-LET particles.</li>
  <li><strong>Equivalent / Effective Dose (H):</strong> Estimates biological dose in Sieverts:
    <div class="formula-box">H = D &times; Q(LET) &nbsp;&nbsp;[in Sieverts, Sv or mSv]</div>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Biological Risk Indicators:</strong> Estimates cellular DNA Double-Strand Breaks (~35 DSBs/cell/Gy), acute fatigue/prodromal probability, Central Nervous System (CNS) HZE hit risk, and utilization of NASA's astronaut career limits (250 mSv Artemis limit and 600 mSv career limit).</li>
</ol>

<h3>4.7 Radiation Visualization</h3>
<p>The platform will provide interactive visualizations to help users understand radiation measurements and estimated radiation conditions.</p>
<p>Planned and implemented visualizations include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Radiation measurements over time and short-term SEP storm forecasts.</li>
  <li>Comparisons between detector readings.</li>
  <li>Primary and secondary radiation contribution estimates across material depth (Depth-Dose &amp; Secondary Neutron/Bremsstrahlung Cascade curves).</li>
  <li>Pre-flight Van Allen Belt trajectory flux and cumulative transit dose profiles.</li>
  <li>Planetary radiation distribution across Moon vs. Mars surface and subsurface habitat locations.</li>
  <li>The 4-Way Spacecraft Engineering Trade-Off Matrix (<em>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</em> comparing Shield A, Shield B, Shield C Bio-Hybrid, and custom configurations).</li>
  <li>Uncertainty and confidence indicators.</li>
  <li>Comparison between measured and simulated results.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.8 Analysis and Reporting (with Neuro-Symbolic AI Co-Pilot)</h3>
<p>Users will be able to review analysis results, compare different scenarios, query the physics engine in natural language via an integrated <strong>Google Gemini Function-Calling Co-Pilot</strong>, and generate reports containing the input data, methods, assumptions, results, and explicit NASA dataset citations.</p>
<p>The system will also identify data-quality issues and explain when insufficient information prevents a reliable estimate.</p>

<h2>5. Project Objectives</h2>
<h3>5.1 Main Objective</h3>
<p>To develop a software prototype that combines multiple radiation detector datasets with physics-based modelling, multi-objective shielding trade-off optimization, and LET-weighted bio-dosimetry to investigate improved radiation dose estimation, the analysis of primary and secondary radiation inside spacecraft, and the estimation of astronaut radiation exposure and biological risk.</p>

<h3>5.2 Specific Objectives</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Identify and collect relevant space radiation datasets from reliable scientific sources (NASA RBSP Van Allen Probes, LRO CRaTER, Curiosity MSL RAD, NOAA GOES/OMNIWeb, and NASA OLTARIS).</li>
  <li>Develop a data-processing module that supports multiple detector measurement formats.</li>
  <li>Implement data validation, unit handling, and measurement comparison.</li>
  <li>Investigate methods for estimating primary and secondary radiation contributions (specifically nuclear spallation neutrons and Bremsstrahlung X-rays).</li>
  <li>Integrate particle transport simulation results into the analysis workflow.</li>
  <li>Develop a data-fusion approach for combining compatible measurements.</li>
  <li>Create interactive visualizations of radiation measurements, orbital Van Allen belt transits, Moon vs. Mars surface comparisons, and estimated spatial distributions.</li>
  <li>Evaluate the prototype against suitable reference measurements or simulation results.</li>
  <li>Document the system's performance, assumptions, and limitations.</li>
  <li>Implement the closed-loop 6-stage operational architecture: <strong>Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor astronaut exposure</strong>.</li>
  <li>Model and evaluate the <strong>3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z Shielding Architecture</strong> (Thin High-Z Stripper + Low-Z Polyethylene/Water Moderator + Gd-157 MXene Nanosheet &amp; Radiotrophic Fungal Melanin Hydrogel Scavenger).</li>
  <li>Optimize the four-way spacecraft engineering constraint (<strong>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</strong>) across candidate shielding stacks.</li>
  <li>Estimate astronaut radiation exposure and associated biological risk using <strong>D = E / m</strong> (Gy), LET-dependent quality factors <strong>Q(LET)</strong>, and Equivalent/Effective Dose in Sieverts (Sv).</li>
</ol>

<h2>6. Proposed System Architecture</h2>
<p>The proposed platform will follow a modular architecture aligned with the six-stage operational workflow (<strong>Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor astronaut exposure</strong>).</p>
<p><strong>System workflow:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Data Sources</li>
  <li>Data Import and Validation (<em>Detect</em>)</li>
  <li>Detector Data Processing (<em>Characterize</em>)</li>
  <li>Particle Transport &amp; Graded-Z Shielding Modelling (<em>Characterize &amp; Protect</em>)</li>
  <li>Data Fusion, Spatial Mapping, and Short-Term Prediction (<em>Map &amp; Predict</em>)</li>
  <li>Spacecraft Trade-Off Optimization &amp; Radiation Exposure Estimation (<em>Protect &amp; Monitor</em>)</li>
  <li>Visualization and Reporting</li>
</ol>

<h3>Main System Modules</h3>
<p><strong>Module 1: Data Collection and Import</strong><br>
Accept datasets from scientific repositories (NASA PDS, CDAWeb, OMNIWeb, OLTARIS), uploaded CSV or JSON files, and simulation outputs.</p>

<p><strong>Module 2: Data Validation and Standardization</strong><br>
Check measurement units, timestamps, missing values, detector metadata, and calibration information. Convert measurements only when the required conversion information is available.</p>

<p><strong>Module 3: Radiation &amp; Graded-Z Shielding Modelling</strong><br>
Use suitable particle transport simulation outputs and Bethe-Bloch / nuclear fragmentation coefficients to investigate particle interactions with spacecraft shielding materials—explicitly modeling primary HZE attenuation, secondary spallation neutron buildup, Bremsstrahlung X-rays, proton recoil moderation, and the 3-layer Bio-Hybrid material stack (including Gd-157 MXene and Fungal Melanin Hydrogel).</p>

<p><strong>Module 4: Data Fusion &amp; Forecasting Engine</strong><br>
Combine compatible detector measurements and model outputs using an appropriate statistical method (inverse-variance weighting, Bayesian estimation, or Kalman filtering) and forecast short-term SEP storm escalation.</p>

<p><strong>Module 5: Radiation Analysis, Trade-Off &amp; Bio-Dosimetry Engine</strong><br>
Produce estimates, comparisons, uncertainty information, primary-secondary contribution analysis, 4-way spacecraft engineering trade-off scores (<em>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</em>), and astronaut exposure metrics (<strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gy &rarr; <strong>Q(LET)</strong> &rarr; Equivalent Dose in mSv and DNA Double-Strand Break risk) where supported by the available inputs.</p>

<p><strong>Module 6: Visualization Dashboard</strong><br>
Display results using interactive charts, orbital and planetary comparison maps, shielding depth-dose profiles, spacecraft diagrams, and interactive spatial visualizations.</p>

<p><strong>Module 7: AI Co-Pilot &amp; Report Generation</strong><br>
Utilize Google Gemini Function Calling to translate natural-language queries into deterministic simulation runs and generate downloadable reports containing analysis results, explicit NASA dataset citations, data sources, and modelling assumptions.</p>

<h2>7. Data Sources and Research Resources</h2>
<p>The project will prioritize publicly available scientific datasets and established space research tools.</p>
<p>Potential resources include:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Resource</th>
      <th>Purpose in the Project</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>NASA Planetary Data System (PDS)</strong></td>
      <td>Access archived planetary mission datasets, including radiation-related observations</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>NASA SPDF / OMNIWeb</strong></td>
      <td>Obtain solar wind, magnetic-field, and energetic particle environment data</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>NASA CDAWeb &amp; Van Allen Probes (RBSP REPT &amp; MagEIS)</strong></td>
      <td>Explore spacecraft and heliophysics datasets, including empirical Inner Proton Belt (1,000–6,000 km) and Outer Relativistic Electron Belt (13,000–25,000 km) flux grids for pre-flight trajectory integration</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>NASA OLTARIS / HZETRN-2020</strong></td>
      <td>Investigate radiation transport, nuclear fragmentation, and Graded-Z shielding analysis</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>ESA SPENVIS</strong></td>
      <td>Study space radiation environments and radiation effects</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>NASA Curiosity RAD (MSL RAD)</strong></td>
      <td>Explore radiation measurements collected on the Martian surface (~240–300 mSv/yr under ~20–34.5 g/cm2 CO2 atmosphere)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter CRaTER</strong></td>
      <td>Explore cosmic-ray-related measurements, archived lunar surface LET spectra (~380–420 mSv/yr), and regolith albedo neutron backscatter data</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>NASA OSDR (RadLab / GeneLab) &amp; ENDF/B-VIII.0 Nuclear Data</strong></td>
      <td>Provide ICRP Q(LET) biological weighting benchmarks, DNA Double-Strand Break kinetics, Gd-157 thermal neutron capture cross-sections (255,000 barns), and radiotrophic melanin attenuation data</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The project will examine the suitability of these resources before integration.</p>
<p>An important distinction is that environmental particle measurements, detector measurements, simulation outputs, and biological risk estimates are different kinds of data. They will not be treated as interchangeable.</p>
<p>For example, OMNIWeb provides information about the near-Earth space environment; it does not directly provide radiation dose measurements inside a spacecraft. Similarly, physical detectors measure energy deposition (Absorbed Dose <strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gray) and LET spectra, from which biological Equivalent Dose (in Sieverts) is estimated using radiation quality factors <strong>Q(LET)</strong>.</p>
<p>The team will document each dataset's origin, measurement type, units, calibration information, and limitations.</p>

<h2>8. Proposed Technology Stack</h2>
<p>The following technologies are proposed for implementation.</p>

