<p>The prototype will not attempt to build a complete 3D Monte Carlo particle transport engine from scratch. Existing scientific tools or suitable precomputed simulation results and deterministic slab transport equations calibrated to NASA OLTARIS are used where appropriate.</p>

<h2>9. Research Methodology</h2>
<p>The project will follow an iterative research and software development process.</p>

<h3>Phase 1: Research and Dataset Identification</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Review space radiation (GCRs, SEPs, Van Allen Belts) and detector technologies.</li>
  <li>Identify suitable public datasets (NASA RBSP, LRO CRaTER, Curiosity MSL RAD, OMNIWeb, OLTARIS).</li>
  <li>Understand available measurement types and their limitations.</li>
  <li>Select a manageable initial dataset and material cross-section library.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> Research notes and selected datasets.</p>

<h3>Phase 2: Data Processing Development (Detect &amp; Characterize)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Build the dataset import module.</li>
  <li>Read CSV and JSON files.</li>
  <li>Validate timestamps, values, and units (MeV, particles/cm2/s/sr, keV/um, g/cm2, Gy, mSv).</li>
  <li>Identify missing or inconsistent metadata.</li>
  <li>Display measurements in a basic dashboard.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> A working multi-dataset import and validation module.</p>

<h3>Phase 3: Radiation Analysis, Graded-Z Shielding, and Trade-Off Modelling (Map &amp; Protect)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Identify suitable reference simulation outputs (NASA OLTARIS / HZETRN-2020).</li>
  <li>Model or import radiation transport results, pre-flight Van Allen Belt trajectories, and Moon vs. Mars planetary surface radiation differences.</li>
  <li>Investigate how shielding materials affect radiation measurements—specifically comparing single-layer High-Z metals against the <strong>3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z stack</strong> (Thin Lead/Al + Polyethylene/Water + <strong>Gd-157 MXene Nanosheets</strong> &amp; <strong>Radiotrophic Fungal Melanin Hydrogel</strong>).</li>
  <li>Develop an initial primary-secondary analysis method (spallation neutrons and Bremsstrahlung X-rays), the 4-way spacecraft trade-off optimizer (<strong>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</strong>), and the physical-to-biological exposure chain (<strong>D = E / m &rarr; Q(LET) &rarr; Sv</strong>).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> An initial radiation, shielding trade-off, and bio-dosimetry analysis module.</p>

<h3>Phase 4: Data Fusion and Prediction (Map &amp; Predict)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Select compatible detector measurements.</li>
  <li>Define the mathematical assumptions and detector response information required.</li>
  <li>Implement a suitable statistical data-fusion method and short-term SEP storm prediction model.</li>
  <li>Compare fused estimates with individual detector estimates.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> A working data-fusion and prediction prototype.</p>

<h3>Phase 5: Visualization and Dashboard (Monitor)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Develop measurement and forecast charts.</li>
  <li>Create detector and planetary (Moon vs. Mars) comparison views.</li>
  <li>Display primary and secondary radiation estimates and the 4-Way Spacecraft Trade-Off Matrix (Shield A vs. Shield B vs. Shield C Bio-Hybrid).</li>
  <li>Build a simplified spacecraft visualization.</li>
  <li>Add uncertainty indicators, astronaut exposure monitors (<strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gy, Equivalent Dose in mSv, DNA DSBs, career limits), and the Gemini natural-language AI Co-Pilot.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> An interactive GadaSpace dashboard (<code>streamlit_app.py</code> and standalone <code>index.html</code>).</p>

<h3>Phase 6: Testing and Evaluation</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Compare individual detector analysis with combined analysis.</li>
  <li>Compare monolithic metal shields against Graded-Z and Bio-Hybrid configurations under mass and power constraints.</li>
  <li>Compare estimates against suitable reference data (NASA LRO CRaTER and Curiosity MSL RAD).</li>
  <li>Test the system with incomplete datasets.</li>
  <li>Measure estimation error and uncertainty.</li>
  <li>Document limitations and future improvements.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> Evaluation results and a final demonstration.</p>

<h2>10. Evaluation and Success Criteria</h2>
<p>The project will evaluate whether the proposed approach provides useful improvements over individual detector analysis and single-layer shielding.</p>
<p>Four progressive approaches will be compared:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Approach</th>
      <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Approach A</strong></td>
      <td>Analysis using one detector dataset and a traditional single-layer heavy metal shield (e.g., Shield A: reduces radiation by 40% but adds 500 kg mass and generates severe secondary neutrons)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Approach B</strong></td>
      <td>Analysis using multiple compatible detector datasets and a single-layer low-Z polymer shield (e.g., Shield B: reduces radiation by 32% and adds only 100 kg mass)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Approach C</strong></td>
      <td>Analysis using multiple detector datasets combined with physics-based particle transport modelling</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Approach D (Full GadaSpace + Bio-Hybrid Engine)</strong></td>
      <td>Full 6-stage pipeline (<em>Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor</em>) combining multi-detector fusion, transport modelling, 4-way trade-off optimization, and the 3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Shield (Shield C: reduces radiation by ~61.5% while adding only 145 kg mass and ~0.05 kW power)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Evaluation Metrics</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Dose Estimation Error:</strong> Compare the system's estimated absorbed dose (<strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gy) and equivalent dose (in mSv) with a suitable reference value (NASA LRO CRaTER lunar baseline of ~380–420 mSv/yr and Curiosity MSL RAD Martian baseline of ~240–300 mSv/yr).</li>
  <li><strong>Primary and Secondary Contribution Error:</strong> Where reference simulation results (NASA OLTARIS) are available, compare estimated primary and secondary contributions (spallation neutrons and Bremsstrahlung X-rays) with the known simulated contributions.</li>
  <li><strong>Spacecraft Trade-Off Efficiency (Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety):</strong> Evaluate candidate shielding architectures using a composite spacecraft systems engineering score that balances dose reduction (%) against structural mass penalty (kg) and active power consumption (kW).</li>
  <li><strong>Data Completeness:</strong> Evaluate how the system behaves when some detector measurements are unavailable.</li>
  <li><strong>Uncertainty Reporting:</strong> Assess whether the system communicates uncertainty and the limitations of its estimates.</li>
  <li><strong>Spatial and Planetary Analysis:</strong> Evaluate whether the system can represent differences between selected spacecraft locations, Van Allen orbital belts, and Moon vs. Mars surface environments using suitable measurement or simulation data.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Proposed Success Criteria</h3>
<p>The prototype will be considered successful if it can:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Import and validate multiple supported dataset formats.</li>
  <li>Identify measurement types and incompatible units.</li>
  <li>Compare detector measurements in a consistent analysis workflow.</li>
  <li>Integrate suitable simulation outputs and reproduce Moon vs. Mars surface dose benchmarks within &plusmn;5%.</li>
  <li>Demonstrate the Secondary Radiation Paradox and optimize the 4-way constraint (<em>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</em>) across traditional and 3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z shields.</li>
  <li>Estimate astronaut radiation exposure and biological risk using <strong>D = E / m</strong> (Gy) and LET-weighted <strong>Q(LET)</strong> equivalent dose (Sv).</li>
  <li>Produce traceable estimates with uncertainty information and explicit NASA dataset citations.</li>
  <li>Visualize radiation measurements and model outputs interactively.</li>
  <li>Demonstrate an evaluation comparing individual and combined analysis methods.</li>
</ul>
<p>The project will not claim that data fusion or novel shielding configurations improve accuracy or protection unless the evaluation results support that conclusion.</p>

<h2>11. Expected Outcomes</h2>
<p>At the end of the project, Team Horizon-X expects to deliver:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>A working web application:</strong> A functional GadaSpace dashboard (FastAPI + Streamlit + standalone interactive <code>index.html</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-detector data processing:</strong> A module for importing and validating different radiation datasets.</li>
  <li><strong>Radiation analysis:</strong> An initial method for analyzing primary and secondary radiation contributions (spallation neutrons and Bremsstrahlung X-rays) where suitable information is available.</li>
  <li><strong>Data fusion &amp; prediction prototype:</strong> A method for combining compatible detector measurements, modelling results, and short-term SEP storm forecasts.</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-objective shielding &amp; Bio-Hybrid trade-off simulator:</strong> Quantitative evaluation of the 3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z shield (Thin High-Z + Polyethylene/Water + Gd-157 MXene &amp; Fungal Melanin Hydrogel) optimized across <em>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Astronaut exposure &amp; biological risk estimator:</strong> Traceable computation of Absorbed Dose (<strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gy), LET Quality Factor <strong>Q(LET)</strong>, Equivalent Dose (Sv/mSv), DNA Double-Strand Breaks, and NASA career exposure limits.</li>
  <li><strong>Interactive visualization &amp; AI Co-Pilot:</strong> Five interactive Plotly scientific charts, simplified spacecraft radiation maps, and a Gemini Function-Calling natural-language query assistant.</li>
  <li><strong>Evaluation report:</strong> A comparison of single-detector, multi-detector, and model-assisted analysis (Approaches A, B, C, and D).</li>
  <li><strong>Technical documentation:</strong> An explanation of the architecture, data sources, methods, assumptions, and limitations.</li>
</ol>

<h2>12. Project Scope and Limitations</h2>
<h3>Included in the Initial Prototype</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Software-based radiation analysis across the 6-stage pipeline (<em>Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor astronaut exposure</em>).</li>
  <li>Publicly available scientific datasets (NASA RBSP, LRO CRaTER, Curiosity MSL RAD, OMNIWeb, OLTARIS).</li>
  <li>Simulated data for testing and demonstration.</li>
  <li>Multi-detector data processing.</li>
  <li>Statistical data fusion and short-term storm prediction.</li>
  <li>Particle transport modelling through existing tools, precomputed outputs, and deterministic 3-layer Graded-Z / Bio-Hybrid slab equations.</li>
  <li>Four-way spacecraft engineering trade-off optimization (<em>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</em>).</li>
  <li>Physical-to-biological exposure estimation (<strong>D = E / m</strong> in Gy to LET-weighted Equivalent Dose in Sv).</li>
  <li>Interactive visualization and AI-assisted natural language querying.</li>
  <li>Research-based evaluation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Outside the Initial Scope</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Building physical radiation detectors or conducting wet-lab chemical synthesis of the Gd-157 MXene and fungal melanin hydrogels during the hackathon.</li>
  <li>Conducting radiation experiments in a laboratory.</li>
  <li>Developing a complete 3D Monte Carlo replacement for established radiation transport software.</li>
  <li>Providing certified clinical astronaut radiation-safety measurements.</li>
  <li>Claiming real-time spacecraft monitoring without an operational data connection.</li>
</ul>
<p>The system will be presented as a research, engineering trade-study, and educational prototype. Its outputs estimate astronaut radiation exposure and associated biological risk and will not be used as certified clinical radiation-safety guidance.</p>

