<h2>14. Potential Impact</h2>
<p>GadaSpace aims to contribute to a more integrated approach to understanding space radiation measurements and optimizing spacecraft protection.</p>
<p>Potential areas of value include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Helping researchers compare measurements from different detector technologies.</li>
  <li>Making radiation datasets (Van Allen Probes, LRO CRaTER, Curiosity MSL RAD, OLTARIS) easier to explore and understand.</li>
  <li>Supporting research into primary and secondary radiation analysis—specifically demonstrating how the Secondary Radiation Paradox arises in high-Z metals and how Graded-Z and Bio-Hybrid materials mitigate it.</li>
  <li>Enabling mission architects to evaluate the critical <strong>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</strong> trade-off for lunar (Artemis) and Martian habitats.</li>
  <li>Demonstrating how scientific modelling, detector measurements, and physical-to-biological dosimetry (<strong>D = E / m &rarr; Q(LET) &rarr; Sv</strong>) can be combined in a closed-loop system (<em>Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor</em>).</li>
  <li>Providing an educational platform for students interested in space science, physics, bio-inspired materials, and software engineering.</li>
</ul>
<p>The broader vision is to explore how data integration and computational modelling can support more informative radiation analysis and mass-efficient protection for future space missions.</p>

<h2>15. Future Development</h2>
<p>After the initial prototype, possible improvements include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Supporting additional detector types and scientific file formats.</li>
  <li>Integrating more mission-specific datasets (such as NASA Artemis Orion HERA and Lunar Gateway HERMES/ERSA sensors).</li>
  <li>Improving particle identification and secondary radiation estimation methods.</li>
  <li>Supporting more detailed 3D CAD spacecraft geometry and shielding models.</li>
  <li>Hardware-in-the-loop CubeSat or high-altitude balloon testing of the physical <strong>3-Layer Bio-Hybrid composite (Thin Al/Pb + Polyethylene + Gd-157 MXene &amp; Radiotrophic Fungal Melanin Hydrogel)</strong> paired with compact solid-state detectors.</li>
  <li>Investigating machine-learning-assisted anomaly detection, where suitable training data is available.</li>
  <li>Developing additional spatial, time-dependent, and personalized astronaut genomic radiosensitivity analysis capabilities.</li>
  <li>Exploring integration with live mission data sources where access is available.</li>
</ul>

<h2>16. Conclusion</h2>
<p>Space radiation is a complex scientific challenge that cannot always be fully understood through a single measurement, a single detector, or a single heavy wall of shielding.</p>
<p>GadaSpace, developed by Team Horizon-X, proposes an integrated software approach that brings together multiple radiation datasets, scientific modelling, statistical analysis, multi-objective shielding trade-off optimization, and interactive visualization.</p>
<p>Rather than developing another standalone radiation detector or optimizing shielding without regard for spacecraft mass and power limits, the project focuses on making existing measurements and modelling results work together across a unified six-stage operational framework: <strong>Detect &rarr; Characterize &rarr; Map &rarr; Predict &rarr; Protect &rarr; Monitor astronaut exposure</strong>.</p>
<p>Through this project—balancing <strong>Protection vs. Mass vs. Power vs. Crew safety</strong>, resolving the Secondary Radiation Paradox via a novel <strong>3-Layer Bio-Hybrid Graded-Z Shield</strong>, and rigorously estimating astronaut exposure from <strong>D = E / m</strong> (Gy) to LET-weighted Equivalent Dose (Sv)—we aim to demonstrate how software engineering, data science, and space physics can be combined to investigate an important challenge in space exploration.</p>
<p>Our vision is simple: turn fragmented radiation measurements into a more complete, understandable, and actionable picture of the space radiation environment.</p>
<hr>

